          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            A
            %em ribbon graph
            (also called
            %em fat graph
            ) is a (typically finite and connected)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/graph"} graph
            equipped with a cyclic ordering on the half edges incident to each vertex.
          %p
            To each ribbon graph, one can associate an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/orientation"} oriented
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/surface"} surface
            with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/boundary"} boundary
            by replacing edges by thin oriented rectangles (ribbons) and vertices by disks, and pasting rectangles to disks according to the chosen cyclic orders at the vertices. (For some illustrations of this, see
            = succeed ".)" do
              %a{:href => "#MulPen"} Mulase-Penkava
          %h2#definition Definition
          %p
            The following definition of an ordinary
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/graph"} graph
            (allowing loops and multiple edges) is not the standard one used for ordinary graphs, but is easily seen to be equivalent to it, while being the right starting point for describing ribbon graphs.
          .num_defn
            %h6#definition_2 Definition
            %p
              A
              %strong
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/graph"} graph
              is a quadruple
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi V
                      %mo ,
                      %mi H
                      %mo ,
                      %mi s
                      %mo ,
                      %mi i
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (V, H, s, i)
            %ul
              %li
                %p
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi V
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
                  is a
                  = succeed "," do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set"} set
                  = succeed ";" do
                    %strong set of vertices
              %li
                %p
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi H
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H
                  is a
                  = succeed "," do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set"} set
                  %strong set of half-edges
              %li
                %p
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi s
                        %mo :
                        %mi H
                        %mo →
                        %mi V
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} s : H \to V
                  is a
                  = succeed "," do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/function"} function
                  %em incident
                  on;
              %li
                %p
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi i
                        %mo :
                        %mi H
                        %mo →
                        %mi H
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i : H \to H
                  is a
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/involution"} involutive
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/function"} function
                  without fixed points, thought of as sending each half-edge to its other half.
            %p
              The set of
              = succeed "s" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cycle"} cycle
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi i
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i
              is the set
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi E
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
              of full edges.
            %p
              A
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homomorphism"} homomorphism
              of graphs is a pair of
              = succeed "s" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/function"} function
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi V
                      %mn 1
                    %mo →
                    %msub
                      %mi V
                      %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V_1 \to V_2
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi H
                      %mn 1
                    %mo →
                    %msub
                      %mi H
                      %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H_1 \to H_2
              commuting with the
              = succeed "-" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi i
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i
              = succeed "-maps." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi s
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} s
          %p
            An edge is a
            %em loop
            if its constituent half-edges are incident on the same vertex.
          .num_defn
            %h6#definition_3 Definition
            %p
              A
              %strong fat graph
              or
              %strong ribbon graph
              is a graph
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi V
                    %mo ,
                    %mi H
                    %mo ,
                    %mi i
                    %mo ,
                    %mi s
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (V,H,i,s)
              equipped with a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bijection"} bijection
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi σ
                    %mo :
                    %mi H
                    %mo →
                    %mi H
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sigma : H \to H
              whose
              = succeed "s" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cycle"} cycle
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi s
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi v
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} s^{-1}(v)
              of half-edges incident on vertices
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi v
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi V
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} v \in V
          %p
            This means that on all sets
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi s
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi v
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} s^{-1}(v)
            of jointly incident half-edges there is induced a cyclic ordering. When drawing ribbon graphs on paper this cyclic ordering is identified with the ordering induced from the standard orientation of the plane.
          %p
            Note that this definition (which appears in
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "#Igusa02"} Igusa 02
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/combinatorial+map"} combinatorial map
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            and the function
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi s
                  %mo :
                  %mi H
                  %mo →
                  %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} s : H \to V
            explicit, instead identifying vertices with the cycles of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi σ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sigma
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi i
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi σ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sigma
            acts
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/transitive+action"} transitively
            on the set of half-edges
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi H
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H
          %h3#boundary_of_a_fat_graph Boundary of a fat graph
          .num_defn
            %h6#definition_4 Definition
            %p Write
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ∂
                    %mo :
                    %mi FatGraph
                    %mo →
                    %mi Graph
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\partial : FatGraph \to Graph
            %p
              for the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functor"} functor
              – called the
              %strong boundary graph functor
              – that sends a fat graph
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Γ
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi V
                    %mo ,
                    %mi H
                    %mo ,
                    %mi s
                    %mo ,
                    %mi i
                    %mo ,
                    %mi σ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma = (V, H, s, i, \sigma)
              to the graph
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ∂
                    %mi Γ
                    %mo =
                    %mrow
                      %mo (
                      %munder
                        %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∐
                        %mrow
                          %mi v
                          %mo ∈
                          %mi V
                      %munder
                        %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∐
                        %mrow
                          %mi h
                          %mo ∈
                          %msup
                            %mi s
                            %mrow
                              %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                              %mn 1
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi v
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                      %msub
                        %mi v
                        %mi h
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                      %mo ,
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %munder
                        %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∐
                        %mrow
                          %mi h
                          %mo ∈
                          %mi H
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                      %msub
                        %mi h
                        %mn 0
                      %mo ,
                      %msub
                        %mi h
                        %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                      %mo ,
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %msub
                        %mi s
                        %mrow
                          %msub
                            %mo ∂
                            %mi Γ
                      %mo ,
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %msub
                        %mi i
                        %mrow
                          %msub
                            %mo ∂
                            %mi Γ
                      %mo )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\partial \Gamma = \left(
                    \\coprod_{v \in V} \coprod_{h \in s^{-1}(v)} \{v_h\},\,
                    \\coprod_{h \in H} \{h_0, h_1\},\,
                    s_{\partial_\Gamma},\,
                    i_{\partial_\Gamma}
                    \\right)
            %p
              with
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi s
                      %mrow
                        %mo ∂
                        %mi Γ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} s_{\partial \Gamma}
              given by
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi s
                      %mrow
                        %mo ∂
                        %mi Γ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi h
                      %mn 0
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %msub
                      %mi v
                      %mi h
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    s_{\partial \Gamma}(h_0) = v_h
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi s
                      %mrow
                        %mo ∂
                        %mi Γ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi h
                      %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mi σ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi v
                      %mi h
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    s_{\partial \Gamma}(h_1) = \sigma(v_h)
            %p
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi i
                      %mrow
                        %mo ∂
                        %mi Γ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i_{\partial \Gamma}
              given by
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi i
                      %mrow
                        %mo ∂
                        %mi Γ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi h
                      %mn 0
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mi i
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi h
                    %msub
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    i_{\partial \Gamma}(h_0) = i(h)_1
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi i
                      %mrow
                        %mo ∂
                        %mi Γ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi h
                      %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mi i
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi h
                    %msub
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mn 0
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    i_{\partial \Gamma}(h_1) = i(h)_0
                    \\,.
          .num_prop
            %h6#proposition Proposition
            %p
              The set of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/boundary"} boundary
              components of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi Σ
                      %mi Γ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma_\Gamma
              is naturally identified with the
              = succeed "s" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cycle"} cycle
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi σ
                      %mo ∘
                      %mi i
                      %mo :
                      %mi H
                      %mo →
                      %mi H
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sigma \circ i : H \to H
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi i
                      %mo ∘
                      %mi σ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i \circ \sigma
          %p
            Write
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                  %mo :
                  %mi FatGraph
                  %mo →
                  %mi Graph
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S : FatGraph \to Graph
            for the evident
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/forgetful+functor"} forgetful functor
            sending fat graphs to their underlying graph.
          .num_prop
            %h6#propositiondefinition Proposition/Definition
            %p
              There is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+transformation"} natural transformation
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi c
                    %mo :
                    %mo ∂
                    %mo →
                    %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    c : \partial \to S
            %p
              from the boundary graph functor to the forgetful functor – called the
              = succeed "," do
                %strong collapse map
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Γ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma
              sends on vertices
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi v
                      %mi h
                    %mo ↦
                    %mi s
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi h
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    v_h \mapsto s(h)
            %p and on half-edges
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi h
                      %mrow
                        %mn 0
                        %mo ,
                        %mn 1
                    %mo ↦
                    %mi h
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    h_{0,1} \mapsto h
                    \\,.
          %h3#further_constructions Further constructions
          %p
            Given any edge
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi e
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} e
            in a graph
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Γ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Γ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi e
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma/e
            by merging the two vertices containing the edge and removing both half edges.
          %p
            A forest
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
            (disjoint union of trees) spans a graph
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Γ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma
            if it contains all of its vertices. One can collapse
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Γ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma
            to
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Γ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma/F
            by collapsing each tree to a separate vertex. Then the edges in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Γ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma/F
            are the edges of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Γ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma
            which do not lie in any tree in
            = succeed ";" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Γ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma/F
            correspond to the sets of leaves of the component trees of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
          %p
            A morphism of graphs
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Γ
                    %mn 0
                  %mo →
                  %msub
                    %mi Γ
                    %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma_0\to\Gamma_1
            is defined as an isomorphism
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Γ
                    %mn 0
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi F
                  %mo →
                  %msub
                    %mi Γ
                    %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma_0/F\to\Gamma_1
            where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
            is a spanning forest of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi Γ
                      %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma_0
          %p
            For a ribbon graph, the set of leaves of any tree inherits a cyclic order. Thus
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Γ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma/F
            has a canonical structure a ribbon graph. This makes the following definition possible: A morphism of ribbon graphs is the morphism of underlying graphs which respects the ordering on the vertices; i.e.
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Γ
                    %mn 0
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi F
                  %mo →
                  %msub
                    %mi Γ
                    %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma_0/F\to\Gamma_1
            is an isomorphism of ribbon graphs. Every endomorphism of a ribbon graph is an automorphism and for any two ribbon graphs
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Γ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Γ
                    %mo ′
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma'
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Aut
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi Γ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Aut(\Gamma)
            acts freely on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Hom
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi Γ
                  %mo ,
                  %mi Γ
                  %mo ′
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Hom(\Gamma,\Gamma')
            on the right and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Aut
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi Γ
                  %mo ′
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Aut(\Gamma')
            acts freely on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Hom
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi Γ
                  %mo ,
                  %mi Γ
                  %mo ′
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Hom(\Gamma,\Gamma')
            from the left.
          %p
            Let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℱ𝒶𝓉
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{Fat}
            denote the category of ribbon graphs and ribbon automorphisms. Strebel proved that the geometric realization
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mi ℱ𝒶𝓉
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} |\mathcal{Fat}|
            of the category of ribbon graphs decomposes into a direct sum of classifying spaces of all
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mapping+class+group"} mapping class group
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msubsup
                    %mi M
                    %mi g
                    %mi s
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M^s_g
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mi ℱ𝒶𝓉
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mo =
                  %munder
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∐
                    %mrow
                      %mi g
                      %mo ,
                      %mi s
                  %msubsup
                    %mi BM
                    %mi g
                    %mi s
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  |\mathcal{Fat}| = \coprod_{g,s} BM^s_g
          %p
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msubsup
                    %mi M
                    %mi g
                    %mi s
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M^s_g
            is a mapping class group of a surface of genus
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi g
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g
            with
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi s
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} s
            punctures. Alternatively, one can instead of the geometric realization take a moduli space of ribbon graphs with metric. A
            %strong metric
            on a ribbon graph is a positive real valued function on the set of edges.
          %h2#properties Properties
          %p For the following we represent a fat graph by
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                a set
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi V
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
                of vertices;
            %li
              %p
                a set
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi H
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H
                of half-edges;
            %li
              %p
                a source map
                = succeed ";" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi s
                        %mo :
                        %mi H
                        %mo →
                        %mi V
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} s : H \to V
            %li
              %p
                an involution
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi i
                      %mo :
                      %mi H
                      %mo →
                      %mi H
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i :  H \to H
                which sends each half-edge to its partner half-edge;
            %li
              %p
                an permutation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi σ
                      %mo :
                      %mi H
                      %mo →
                      %mi H
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sigma : H \to H
                which gives the cyclic order on the edges.
          %h3#geometric_realization Geometric realization
          %p
            For
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Γ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma
            a fat graph, write
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Σ
                    %mi Γ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma_\Gamma
            for the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/surface"} surface
            that it defines.
          .num_prop
            %h6#proposition_2 Proposition
            %p
              The
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/genus"} genus
              of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi Σ
                      %mi Γ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma_\Gamma
              is
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi g
                    %mo =
                    %mfrac
                      %mn 1
                      %mn 2
                    %mrow
                      %mo (
                      %mn 2
                      %mo −
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                      %mi V
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                      %mo +
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                      %mi E
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                      %mo −
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                      %mi boundary
                      %mi components
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                      %mo )
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    g = \frac{1}{2}
                    \\left(
                    2 - \vert V\vert + \vert E\vert - \vert boundary components\vert
                    \\right)
                    \\,.
          .num_prop
            %h6#proposition_3 Proposition
            %p
              For every surface
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
              with boundary, there is a fat graph
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Γ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma
              and a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homeomorphism"} homeomorphism
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi S
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mo ≃
                    %msub
                      %mi Σ
                      %mi Γ
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    S \stackrel{\simeq}{\to}  \Sigma_\Gamma
                    \\,.
          %h3#moduli_space_of_surfaces__classifying_spaces_of_mapping_class_groups Moduli space of surfaces / classifying spaces of mapping class groups
          %p
            Write
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msubsup
                    %mi FatGraph
                    %mn 3
                    %mi c
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} FatGraph^c_3
            for the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/full+subcategory"} full subcategory
            of fat graphs on the connected fat graphs for which every vertex has valence at least 3. Write
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %msubsup
                    %mi FatGraph
                    %mn 3
                    %mi c
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mo ∈
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \vert FatGraph^c_3 \vert \in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Top"} Top
            for the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+realization"} geometric realization
            of this
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Σ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma
            a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/surface"} surface
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Γ
                    %mi Sigma
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma_Sigma
            for its
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mapping+class+group"} mapping class group
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi B
                  %msub
                    %mi Γ
                    %mi Σ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B \Gamma_\Sigma
            for the corresponding
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classifying+space"} classifying space
          .num_theorem
            %h6#theorem Theorem
            %p
              There is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/weak+homotopy+equivalence"} weak homotopy equivalence
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %msubsup
                      %mi FatGraph
                      %mn 3
                      %mi c
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mo ≃
                    %munder
                      %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∐
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                        %mi Σ
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mi B
                    %msub
                      %mi Γ
                      %mi Σ
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo ,
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\vert FatGraph^c_3 \vert \simeq
                    \\coprod_{[\Sigma]} B \Gamma_\Sigma
                    \\,,
            %p
              where on the right thr coproduct ranges over isomorphism classes of orientable, closed 2-dimensional manifolds with
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                    %mo ≥
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n \geq 1
              marked points, except those for which
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi g
                    %mo ,
                    %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 0
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (g,n) = (0,1)
              or
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi g
                      %mo ,
                      %mi n
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn 0
                      %mo ,
                      %mn 2
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (g, n) = (0,2)
          %p
            In various guises this theorem has been proven by
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Costello"} Costello
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Kontsevich92"} Kontsevich92
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Igusa02"} Igusa02
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Penner"} Penner
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#Strebel"} Strebel
          %p
            The restriction to valence
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ≥
                  %mn 3
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \geq 3
            can be dropped:
          .num_theorem
            %h6#theorem_2 Theorem
            %p
              There is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/weak+homotopy+equivalence"} weak homotopy equivalence
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %msup
                      %mi FatGraph
                      %mi c
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mo ≃
                    %mi B
                    %mi U
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∐
                    %mi B
                    %mi U
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %munder
                      %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∐
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                        %mi Σ
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mi B
                    %msub
                      %mi Γ
                      %mi Σ
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo ,
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\vert FatGraph^c \vert \simeq
                    B U(1) \coprod B U(1) \coprod_{[\Sigma]} B \Gamma_\Sigma
                    \\,,
            %p
              where again on the right the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coproduct"} coproduct
              ranges over
              = succeed "es" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isomorphism+class"} isomorphism class
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                    %mo ≥
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n \geq 1
              marked points, except those for which
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi g
                    %mo ,
                    %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 0
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (g,n) = (0,1)
              or
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi g
                      %mo ,
                      %mi n
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn 0
                      %mo ,
                      %mn 2
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (g, n) = (0,2)
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi B
                    %mi U
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B U(1)
              corespond to these two exceptional cases.
          %p
            This has been shown in (
            %a{:href => "#Godin"}> Godin
            ). One of the
            = succeed "-summands" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi B
                    %mi U
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B U(1)
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#Igusa02"} Igusa02
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#Kupers"} Kupers, theorem 3.59
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stringy+weight+system"} stringy weight system
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/graph"} graph
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/directed+graph"} directed graph
              %ul
                %li
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/directed+n-graph"} directed n-graph
            %li
              %p
                %strong ribbon graph
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+map"} topological map
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/combinatorial+map"} combinatorial map
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ribbon+category"} ribbon category
          %h2#references References
          %p
            Survey in the context of
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+topology"} string topology
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Sander+Kupers"}> Sander Kupers
              , Chapter 3 of:
              %em String topology operations
              MSc thesis (2011) (
              %a{:href => "https://dspace.library.uu.nl/handle/1874/206062"}> dspace:1874/206062
              )
          %p
            Survey in the context of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/weight+systems"} weight systems
            on
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/chord+diagrams"} chord diagrams
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jacobi+diagrams"} Jacobi diagrams
          %ul
            %li#ChmutovDuzhinMostovoy11
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Sergei+Chmutov"}> Sergei Chmutov
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Sergei+Duzhin"} Sergei Duzhin
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jacob+Mostovoy"} Jacob Mostovoy
              = succeed "," do
                %em Introduction to Vassiliev knot invariants
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/1103.5628"} arxiv:1103.5628
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1017/CBO9781139107846"} doi:10.1017/CBO9781139107846
          %p Original articles:
          %ul
            %li#Kontsevich92
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Maxim+Kontsevich"}> Maxim Kontsevich
                ,
                %em Intersection theory on the moduli space of curves and the matrix Airy function
                , Commun.
              %p Math. Phys. (1992), no. 147, 1-23.
            %li#Igusa02
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kiyoshi+Igusa"}> Kiyoshi Igusa
                ,
                %em Higher Franz Reidemeister torsion
                , IP Studies in Advanced Mathematics, American Mathematical Society, 2002.
            %li#Igusa04
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kiyoshi+Igusa"}> Kiyoshi Igusa
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Graph cohomology and Kontsevich cycles
                %strong 43
                (2004), n. 6, p. 1469-1510,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "http://www.ams.org/mathscinet-getitem?mr=2005d:57028"} MR2005d:57028
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1016/j.top.2004.03.004"} doi
            %li#Strebel
              %p
                K. Strebel,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Quadratic Differentials
                %a{:href => "https://www.ams.org/mathscinet-getitem?mr=86a:30072"} MR86a:30072
            %li#Penner
              %p
                R. C. Penner,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em The decorated Teichmüller space of punctured surfaces
                %strong 113
                (2) (1987) 299–339.
                %a{:href => "https://www.ams.org/mathscinet-getitem?mr=89h:32044"} MR89h:32044
            %li
              %p
                John Harer,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em The cohomology of the moduli space of curves
                = succeed "," do
                  %strong 1337
            %li#MulPen
              %p
                M. Mulase, M. Penkava,
                %em Ribbon graphs, quadratic differentials on Riemann surfaces, and algebraic curves defined over
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mover
                          %mi ℚ
                          %mo ¯
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \overline{\mathbb{Q}}
                %strong 2
                (1998), no. 4, 875–919. (
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/math-ph/9811024"}> math-ph/9811024
                )
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Maxim+Kontsevich"}> Maxim Kontsevich
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Feynman diagrams and low-dimensional topology
                = succeed "," do
                  %strong 120
                %a{:href => "http://www.ihes.fr/~maxim/TEXTS/Feynman%20%20diagrams%20and%20low-dimensional%20topology.pdf"} pdf
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ib+Madsen"}> Ib Madsen
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Michael+Weiss"} Michael Weiss
                = succeed "," do
                  %em
                    The stable moduli space of Riemann surfaces:
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Mumford%27s+conjecture"} Mumford's conjecture
                %strong 165
                (2007), no. 3, 843–941,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "http://www.ams.org/mathscinet-getitem?mr=2009b:14051"} MR2009b:14051
                %a{:href => "http://dx.doi.org/10.4007/annals.2007.165.843"} doi
            %li
              %p
                J. Conant,
                = succeed "," do
                  %span.newWikiWord
                    Karen Vogtmann
                    %a{:href => "/nlab/new/Karen+Vogtmann"} ?
                = succeed "," do
                  %em On a theorem of Kontsevich
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/math/0208169"} math.QA/0208169
            %li
              %p
                Gabriele Mondello,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Riemann surfaces, ribbon graphs and combinatorial classes
                %a{:href => "http://www1.mat.uniroma1.it/people/mondello/ricerca/papers/ober-definitive.pdf"} pdf
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/0705.1792"} arxiv:0705.1792
            %li#Godin
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Veronique+Godin"}> Veronique Godin
                ,
                %em Higher string topology operations
                (2007)(
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/0711.4859"}> arXiv:0711.4859
                )
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kevin+Costello"}> Kevin Costello
                \.
                = succeed "," do
                  %em A dual version of the ribbon graph decomposition of moduli space
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.2140/gt.2007.11.1637"} doi
          %p Further discussion
          %ul
            %li
              MathOverflow:
              %a{:href => "http://mathoverflow.net/questions/51978/why-does-ribbon-graph-cohomology-compute-cohomology-of-mcg"} why does ribbon graph cohomology compute cohomology of mapping class group
        .revisedby
